  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nThe upper-level shortwave ridge overhead today will give way  to a \ntrough and deepening low pressure system making its way into the \ncentral CONUS. The low itself is expected to lift northeastward \ntomorrow from the Southern High Plains to the Upper Midwest which \nshould direct most forcing north and west of our forecast area, but \na spoke of the shortwave energy pivoting overhead during the \nafternoon and evening shows at least modest height falls over the \nArkLaMiss Delta region. Without prolonged warm, moist advection in \nadvance of this system's cold frontal passage, forecast instability \nis in the range of 500-1500 J/kg over northeast LA during the \ndaylight hours tomorrow but decreasing to around 400-700 J/kg as the \nfront moves further east into Mississippi after dark. There is a \nmodest 40kts of deep-layer shear expected across the front, but less \nfavorable instability suggests only low potential for pre-frontal \nconvection to develop for discreet supercells beyond northeast LA in \nthe afternoon hours. The primary hazards will be wind squalls with \nline segments into the early morning hours, heavy rainfall rates. \nWeak tornadoes cannot be ruled out. Heaviest rain rates could pose \nan isolated flash flood threat in low-lying or urban areas, but the \nprogressive movement of the front should limit an organized flooding \nthreat. The Marginal severe storm risk was expanded slightly to the \neast with this forecast cycle, and the \"Limited\" flash flood threat \ngraphic was trimmed to primarily the Delta where initial rain rates \nand a less organized front could see the rain accumulate a little \nfaster in the evening hours.\n\nThis weather should clear by Wednesday, but showers could linger in \nthe wake of the front across eastern Mississippi Wednesday morning. \nThe pattern will reinforce mostly dry weather Wednesday into early \nnext week. Building high pressure over the weekend will push \ntemperatures below normal across our forecast area. High \ntemperatures Saturday through next Monday should be mostly in the \nmid to upper 60s or near 70 degrees, while overnight low from Friday \nnight onwards will dip into the 30s and 40s. Frost seems likely in \nthat time frame with any periods of particularly calm overnight \nwinds and mostly clear skies. Will continue to monitor trends, but \nwe are in the time of year where frost and light freeze are normal \nand the growing season has ended already. /NF/",
